@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  supported child host, parent adapter, child host, parent model,
}
@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  starter tools, steering message, turn,
}

component AgentConfig {
  goal {
    Load and validate the spike's agent configuration so that every other
    component receives an agent definition whose child host, model and
    thinking level come from one model tier and whose skills are absolute
    folders.
  }

  state {
    The configuration is two layers. The *agent file*, .agents/subzero.yaml
    under the project root, holds a *skills map* from skill name to
    *skill folder*, a *models map* and an *agents map* of
    named agents, each an *agent entry*. The *global file*,
    ~/.agents/subzero.yaml, whose path *SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG* may name instead,
    holds only a models map with its
    tiers map and its parallel map, the *routing block* that the agent file may
    also hold and the child host options, which the agent file may not hold. A
    hosts map under the key hosts gives the child host options of a child host,
    from the name of a child host to a map. AgentConfig keeps each map as
    written and never reads inside one, because the child driver of that child
    host owns what its options mean. A *shared file* is the agent file when it and
    the global file are one file, symbolic links followed, as in a project
    whose project root is the home folder.

    The *merged configuration* is the global file and the agent file combined
    by name: each model tier by its name, each parallel limit by its model
    and each key of the routing block by its key. An entry of the agent
    file replaces the entry of the global file with that name whole, and an
    entry that the agent file does not name is taken from the global file. An
    agent file that names a model tier or the model of a parallel limit with no
    value holds a *removal entry*, which takes it out of the merged
    configuration.
    The agent file may hold an *enabled switch*, and an agent file whose enabled
    switch is false switches subzero off for its project.

    The models map holds a *tiers map* and a *parallel map*. The tiers map
    gives each *model tier* by name a
    child host, which the agent file writes under the key host, a *model* and a
    *thinking level*. The child host, the model and the thinking level live
    only in a model tier or a tier override, and never directly in an
    agent entry.

    The *medium tier* is the model tier named medium. It is what a request runs
    on when it names no model tier and the agent entry names none of its own,
    so the merged configuration must hold it.

    An agent entry has a *description*, the names of its skills, a *tool list*
    and a *timeout*, and may name a model tier as its own tier and hold a
    *tier override* for any model tier. The entries named general and fallback
    have
    no description. A tier override replaces, for that agent only, any of the
    child host, the model and the thinking level of the model tier it names,
    and leaves the rest of that model tier as it is.

    An *agent definition* is one agent entry resolved at one model tier: the
    tier that the request names, or else the entry's own tier, or else the
    medium tier. It has the description, the skills, the tool list and the
    timeout of the entry, and the child host, the model and the thinking level
    of that model tier after the entry's tier override for it. It is named
    after the entry, and named <entry>:<tier> when the request names a
    model tier.

    The parallel map gives the *parallel limit* of a model, keyed by the whole
    model, a positive whole number that is the most child runs that one
    process runs at the same time on that model. Every agent definition whose
    model is that model shares its parallel limit, whichever model tier names
    it, and a model with no entry in the parallel map has no parallel limit.

    A *model endpoint* is the part of a model before its first slash. The
    *endpoints map* is the key endpoints that earlier versions used to give a
    parallel limit to each model endpoint, one *endpoints entry* for each, and
    it is no longer part of the agent file or the global file.

    The timeout is a required number of seconds. It limits the wall-clock time
    of the child run of each turn from its spawn to its settlement, including
    skill verification, starts again each time a steering message is
    delivered into that child run, and does not run while the child run is
    Pending.

    The merged configuration must hold an entry named general, the
    *general agent*, which takes work that names no agent or names general, and
    whose tool list and timeout tiered work uses. It must also hold an entry
    named fallback, the *fallback agent*, which takes the work of a name other than
    general that no agent entry of the merged configuration holds. Its tool list
    can be limited to
    tools that only read, and the init with a model writes it that way. Neither
    the general agent nor the fallback agent is chosen by its description or
    offered to a parent model for routing.

    A *built-in agent* is the general agent or the fallback agent when the
    agents map does not hold it. The built-in general agent has the tool list
    for a general agent and the built-in fallback agent has the tool list that
    only reads, both from the starter tools that the caller gives for the child
    host of the medium tier, as the init with a model writes them. A built-in
    agent has no skills and a timeout of 1200 seconds, and an entry of the
    agents map with the same name replaces it. The medium tier, the general agent
    and the fallback agent that
    the init with a model adds to the agent file are the *starter entries*.

    A *routable tier* is a model tier whose child host, after the tier override
    of the general agent, is a supported child host, or any model tier when the
    caller gives no supported child hosts. The medium tier is a routable tier
    on the same terms as any other, and a front end that routes by tier word
    leaves out a routable tier that has no tier word.

    A *tier word* is how a compound-engineering skill names the model of a
    generic subagent. The tier word cheapest capable maps to the model tier
    small, mid-tier maps to medium, and session model maps to large. The
    tier word session model is the name that compound-engineering uses for the
    model tier large. It is not a fallback to the model of the parent session,
    and
    no request runs on it unless it names it.

    A *working directory* is the directory in which a session of a parent host
    or a command was started.

    A *change set* is what a guided setup asks to change in one file: set
    a model tier to a model and a thinking level, remove a model tier, set the
    parallel limit of a model, or remove it. It changes each
    model tier and each model at most once, and each model tier that
    it sets runs on the child host that its caller gives. A *setup scope* says
    which file a change set is for: the global file or the agent file, which
    is the default. The
    *tier dependents* of a model tier are the agent entries that name it as
    their own tier or hold a tier override for it, the general agent and the
    fallback agent included.

    A *setup preview* of a change set holds the change set in a canonical
    order, each *refused change* with its reason and the agent entries that cause
    it, each parallel limit that the write drops because no model tier or
    tier override uses its model after it, each model tier and parallel limit
    that the change set sets or removes and that the agent file of this project
    overrides, when the setup scope is the global file, the endpoints map that
    the write removes, and a diff and a summary of the write. It also holds a
    *preview fingerprint* over the text of the file of its setup scope or its
    absence, the change set, the setup scope and the child host of the write.

    A *tier text* is the model tier that a parent model writes in a request,
    which may be empty. An *agent alias* is a name that a parent model may
    write for the general agent: delegate and general are the agent aliases.
  }

  logic {
    On a read of the child host options for a guided setup, read only the
    global file, whatever the agent file holds and also when the project is off,
    and validate it as a global layer is validated on load, except that a missing
    medium tier is no error. Return the
    child host options of the child host that the caller names, or the load
    error that names the global file, which ends the flow.

    On load, read the agent file first. When it holds an enabled switch that is
    false, stop with the *off error* before the global file is read. Otherwise
    read the global file when it exists, validate the global file as a layer
    that holds only a models map with its tiers map and its parallel map, the
    routing block and the child host options, validate the agent file as a layer
    against the *agent-file schema*, which consists only of the validation rules in
    this
    AgentConfig contract, merge the two layers by name, fill each default
    agent, and validate the merged configuration: every model tier, every agent
    entry,
    the general agent, the fallback agent and the medium tier included, and every
    tier override. Resolve each skill folder relative to the directory of the
    agent file into an absolute path. Take out of the merged configuration a
    parallel limit that only the global file holds and whose model no
    model tier or tier override uses, without an error. A shared file is read once,
    as the global layer, and the
    agent file layer is then empty, as if the agent file were missing, so
    that its enabled switch is not read and a key meant only for the agent
    file is a global file error. The flow returns each agent entry, each
    model tier, the child host options and the names of each routable tier to its
    caller, or stops with the off error when
    the agent file is missing and the merged configuration holds no medium tier,
    or with a *load error* that names the file it comes from, the global file
    or the agent file, and the offending entry or model tier, or names the
    missing general agent, fallback agent or medium tier, or says that a
    file is unreadable or malformed or holds an endpoints map. One invalid
    model tier or agent entry fails the whole load.

    On a request for an agent, resolve the agent entry for the agent name, or
    the general agent when the request names no agent or an agent alias. A name
    that no agent entry of the merged configuration holds, a built-in agent
    included, stops the flow with the unknown agent error. Then choose the
    model tier: the tier the request
    names, or else the entry's own tier, or else the medium tier. Apply the
    entry's tier override for that tier and return the agent definition, which
    ends the flow. When the chosen tier is not configured, or its child host
    after the override is not a supported child host, return the tier error and
    end the flow.

    On a tier text, return the model tier it maps to, which ends the flow: a
    configured model tier name, or tier: followed by one, maps to that
    model tier, and a tier word maps to its model tier. An empty tier text maps
    to no model tier, and any other tier text is an *unmatched tier text*,
    which the caller treats as an empty tier text.

    On a read of the *setup state*, read the global file and the agent file,
    reading a shared file once as the global file, and return each model tier of
    the merged configuration with its tier
    dependents, its model and the file it comes from, and, when the
    agent file overrides it, the global file's model tier, each parallel limit
    with the model tiers that use its model, the file it comes from, whether
    only a tier override uses it and whether no model tier uses it, and each
    endpoints entry of either file with its parallel limit, the file it comes
    from and each model that belongs to its model endpoint and that its old
    limit covered, as the load error lists them, which ends the flow. A missing
    agent file gives a
    setup state with no file. A file that fails to load only for a missing
    medium tier, a parallel limit that no model tier uses or an endpoints map
    gives the setup state and that load error as what the write repairs, and
    any other load error gives a refused setup state that holds the load error
    unchanged.

    On a change set, refuse it with a *change set error* when it is malformed
    or changes a model tier or a model twice, and with the load error
    when the setup state is refused, which ends the flow. Otherwise build the
    setup preview. Refuse the removal of the medium tier, of a model tier that
    is not configured, and of a model tier that has tier dependents, naming
    every one of them. Refuse a parallel limit for a model that no
    model tier or tier override uses, and refuse the
    whole change set when no medium tier would remain in the merged
    configuration. When the setup scope is the agent file and the agent file is
    a shared file, refuse the whole change set with a refused change that names
    the shared file, and write nothing, and likewise refuse to add the starter
    entries to a shared file. When the setup scope is the agent file,
    removing a model
    tier or a parallel limit that only the global file holds writes a removal
    entry. When the setup scope is the global file, also build the merged
    configuration of this project with the changed global file, refuse a
    change after which that merged configuration would lack a model tier that
    an agent entry of this project names as its own tier or holds a tier
    override for, and keep a load error of this project that the change would
    cause.
    Drop the parallel limit of each model that no model tier or tier override
    uses after the change, keep one that only a tier override uses, remove the
    endpoints map from the file of the setup scope, and load the result as the
    merged configuration, keeping its load error when it would not load. A
    change set adds no starter entry. Return the setup preview and
    write nothing, which ends the flow.

    On an apply of a change set with a preview fingerprint, build its
    setup preview again from the file of its setup scope as it is now. Refuse
    with a
    *stale preview error* when the preview fingerprint differs, and refuse
    when the setup preview holds a refused change or would not load, writing
    nothing. Write nothing when nothing changes, and otherwise write the result
    and return the setup preview with whether it wrote, which ends the flow.
    When the file system refuses that write, leave that file as it was
    and refuse with a *write error* that names the file and the cause the
    file system gave, which ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    A model tier must be rejected with a load error that names it when it has
    no child host, no model or no thinking level. A tier override must be
    rejected with a load error that names its agent and its model tier when it
    names a model tier that is not configured or holds anything other than a
    child host, a model or a thinking level. An agent entry must be rejected,
    naming it, when its own tier is not a configured model tier, or when it
    holds a child host, a model or a thinking level directly.

    The merged configuration must be rejected with a load error that names the
    medium tier when the agent file exists and neither file holds a model tier
    named medium, except that an agent file whose enabled switch is false is off
    without it. When the caller gives no starter tools to a call that returns an
    agent definition, a general agent or fallback agent that the agents map does
    not hold must be rejected with a load error that names it. A call that
    lists only the tier names, the routable tiers or the routing block needs no
    tool list, and fills each built-in agent without one.

    The global file must be rejected with a load error that names the global
    file and the key when it holds anything other than a models map with a
    tiers map and a parallel map, the routing block or the child host options,
    the enabled switch, a skills map and an agents map included. The key
    hosts must be a map whose every value is a map, and anything else gives a
    load error that names the global file, the key hosts and, for a value that
    is not a map, its child host. The load checks
    nothing inside a map, because a malformed one must not break a project that
    never uses that child host, and the child driver reports it when it would
    start a child. An agent file that holds the key hosts must be rejected with
    a load error that names the agent file, because a repository must not choose
    the code that runs in the children of its user, and a shared file is
    read as the global file, so its hosts key is accepted. The process
    environment, SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG, HOME and PATH included, is trusted, so
    AgentConfig honors the child host options of whichever file is the global
    file. An
    empty SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG counts as unset, a missing global file is no layer
    and no error, and an
    unreadable or malformed global file must be rejected with a load error that
    names it. A removal entry is accepted only in the agent file. The enabled
    switch must be true or false.

    Each load error must name the file it comes from, the global file or the
    agent file. An agent entry whose own tier or tier override names a model
    tier is checked against the model tiers of the merged configuration, and a
    model tier that a removal entry took out is not configured. A general agent
    or fallback agent in the agents map replaces the built-in agent of that name
    and nothing else, so an agents map with only a reviewer entry still gets
    both built-in agents.

    AgentConfig takes the supported child hosts from its caller, which gets
    them from Kernel, and AgentConfig names no child host itself. When its
    caller gives no supported child hosts, AgentConfig checks no child host. A
    model tier whose child host is not a supported child host still loads and
    is not a routable tier.

    The general agent and the fallback agent must each be rejected with a
    load error that starts with its name when it holds a description, because
    neither is chosen by its description.

    A parallel limit must be rejected with a load error that names its model
    when it is not a positive whole number, or when no model tier or tier
    override of the merged configuration uses its model, except a
    parallel limit that only the global file holds, which is ignored in a
    project whose model tiers do not use its model.

    An agent file or a global file that holds an endpoints map must be
    rejected with a load error that names that file, says that the
    endpoints map was replaced by the parallel map, lists for each
    endpoints entry the models that belong to its model endpoint and that its
    old limit covered, which for the agent file are those of the model tiers
    and tier overrides of the merged configuration and for the global file
    are those of its own model tiers, and says that
    guided setup repairs the file. An endpoints entry is never turned into a
    parallel limit without a write that the person confirms.

    An agent entry must be rejected, naming it, when it has no timeout or its
    timeout is not a positive number of seconds.

    An agent entry that names a skill missing from the skills map must be
    rejected with the agent and the skill both named.

    The fallback agent must keep the tool list that the agent file gives it, at
    every model tier, and its tool list must not change the tool list of the
    general agent. AgentConfig names no tool, because tool names belong to the
    child host.

    A skill folder that does not exist on disk must still load. The missing
    folder is reported only when a child is launched.

    A request that resolves an agent at a model tier that is not configured, or
    whose child host after the tier override is not a supported child host,
    must return a *tier error* that names the tier it resolved to, lists every
    configured model tier and, when the child host is the cause, names that
    child host and every supported child host, and it must not fail the load.
    The listing of each routable tier names a model tier that the
    general agent can run, for the tool description and the hook, and whether
    any agent runs at a model tier is decided by the child host of its resolved
    agent definition.

    AgentConfig must read the agent file and the global file again on each call
    that asks for an agent definition or a model tier, so that an edit to
    either is picked up without a restart.

    AgentConfig must read only the agent file and the global file, and it
    writes the agent file only to add the starter entries and the file of the
    setup scope only to apply a change set, as its interface describes. It
    must not read a personal override file, write *subzero.lock* or ask for
    *repo trust* in the spike, because those belong to the agent definition
    format of a later version.

    When it writes a file, AgentConfig must leave every entry that the
    write does not change as it was, and leave each line that the write does
    not change as it was, except when adding to an empty or *flow-style map*
    requires rewriting that map. Adding the starter entries adds only the
    medium tier, the general agent or the fallback agent when the agent file
    does not hold it yet. A change set may replace a model tier, remove a
    model tier or a parallel limit together with the comment lines directly
    above it, remove several entries of one map in the same write, set a
    parallel limit, creating the parallel map when it is missing, remove the
    endpoints map together with the comment lines directly above it, and for
    the agent file write a removal entry for a model tier or a parallel limit
    that only the global file holds. AgentConfig must
    write nothing when the file exists and does not
    load for a reason other than missing starter entries or, for a change set,
    a missing medium tier, a parallel limit that no model tier uses or an
    endpoints map, and must write only entries that load. A write for the
    global file creates its folder
    when it is missing.

    An apply must write a change set only with the preview fingerprint of its
    setup preview, and must refuse with the stale preview error and write
    nothing when the file of its setup scope, the setup scope or the change set
    differs from what was previewed, including a file created or removed since.
    It writes through a *temporary file* next to that file, created exclusively
    so that its own path never names an existing file or symbolic link, removed on
    any
    failure and then renamed over that file, so that a call that reads the
    file again sees the old file or the new one and never a part of one. When
    that file is a symbolic link, every link is followed to the file it
    resolves to, the temporary file is next to that file, the rename replaces
    that file and each symbolic link stays as it was.

    AgentConfig resolves the *project root* from *SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR* first,
    then from the roots that the parent adapter reports, and for the command
    line from its working directory. It must never use the working directory of
    the process that runs it, except for the command line. It keeps no
    project root between calls, because DelegationService asks once for each
    DelegationService and hands the answer to the other components. It finds
    the agent file under the project root that its caller hands over.

    A caller may ask AgentConfig to resolve the project root from the roots
    that its parent adapter reports alone, and then SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR is not
    read.

    An empty SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR counts as unset. When the parent adapter
    reports several roots, the first one reported wins. The project root must
    be an absolute path with its symbolic links resolved. When no source gives
    a project root, AgentConfig must return an *unresolved root error* that
    names SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR as the setting to fix, and must resolve again on
    the next call.

    AgentConfig finds the global file at the path that SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG
    names, or else in the home folder of the user, and no test reads the home
    folder of a developer.
  }

  cases {
    A file with the medium tier set to the child host pi, the model
    r9700/Qwen3.8 and the thinking level medium, a general agent, a
    fallback agent and one agent entry with no tier of its own, loads, and that
    agent definition has that child host, model and thinking level and absolute
    skill folder entries.

    With the model tiers medium, small and large configured, the general agent
    resolves to four agent definitions: general on the medium tier, and
    general:small, general:medium and general:large on the tier each names.

    A reviewer entry whose own tier is small resolves to the small model tier
    when the request names no tier, and to the large model tier, named
    reviewer:large, when the request names large. A tier override of the
    reviewer for large that sets only the model keeps the child host and the
    thinking level of large.

    An agent entry that holds a model directly gives a load error that names
    it, and so does a tier override that names a model tier that is not
    configured.

    A file that holds an agent entry but no general agent gets the default
    general agent, and a file with a general agent and no fallback agent gets
    the built-in fallback agent. When the caller gives no starter tools, a
    call that returns an agent definition gives a load error that names the
    missing agent. An agent file with no model tier named medium, when the
    global file holds none either, gives a load error that names the
    medium tier.

    A global file with the model tiers small, medium and large and a parallel
    limit for the model local/qwen3-coder-30b of its medium tier, and an agent
    file with only a medium tier on local/qwen3-coder-30b and a parallel limit
    for it, give a merged configuration with the agent file's medium tier
    whole, the global small and large, and the agent file's parallel limit for
    local/qwen3-coder-30b. With a global file that holds a medium
    tier and no agent file, the project is on, and the general agent resolves
    to a built-in agent on the medium tier with the tool list for a general
    agent from the starter tools that the caller gives and a timeout of 1200
    seconds, and the fallback agent to a built-in agent whose tool list only
    reads. With a global file that holds
    no medium tier and no agent file, the project is off with the off error.
    An agent file that holds only an enabled switch of false is off with the
    off error, whether or not a global file exists, and a broken global file
    does not reach it.

    A global file that holds an agents map gives a load error that names the
    global file and the agents key, in every project that is on. A removal
    entry for large in the agent file takes large out of the merged
    configuration, and an agent entry whose own tier is large then gives a load
    error that names the agent file. A global parallel limit for a model
    that no model tier of the project uses is ignored in that project.

    A global file whose hosts key holds, for the child host pi, a map with a
    list of extensions gives that map as written to its caller, in every
    project that is on, and a map for a child host that no caller starts is kept
    and used by no one. A map that holds nothing valid for its child driver
    still loads, and the child driver reports it when it would start a child. A
    global file named by SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG gives its child host options
    like the file at ~/.agents/subzero.yaml, and an agent file that holds the
    key hosts gives a load error that names the agent file. In a
    project whose project root is the home folder, the shared file is read once
    as the global file, so its models map and its child host options both load, and
    a change set for the agent file is refused naming the shared file with
    nothing written. A change set for a file that is a symbolic link into a
    dotfiles folder changes the file the link resolves to, and the symbolic link
    still names it afterwards.

    A model tier large with the child host claude, when only pi is a
    supported child host, loads, is not a routable tier, and a request for it
    gives the tier error that names claude and pi. When the caller gives no
    supported child hosts, large is a routable tier.

    A name that is not configured gives an unknown agent error that lists every
    configured agent entry, the built-in agents included. AgentConfig only
    reports the error, and DelegationService decides whether the fallback agent
    runs instead.

    Adding the starter entries to an agent file that holds general and fallback
    agents and no medium tier adds the medium tier and leaves every existing
    entry as it was. Adding them where no agent file exists creates the file
    with all three.

    With no SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR and no root reported by the parent adapter, the
    project root is not resolved and the caller gets the unresolved root error.

    Two agent definitions on the model tiers medium and low, which both name
    the model r9700/Qwen3.8 whose parallel limit is 1, are both given that
    parallel limit of 1, and an agent definition on a model with no
    parallel limit is given none. A parallel limit for r9700/Qwen3.9 that no
    model tier or tier override uses gives a load error that names
    r9700/Qwen3.9.

    An agent file whose endpoints map gives r9700 a parallel limit of 1, with
    model tiers on r9700/Qwen3.8 and r9700/Qwen3-4B, gives a load error that
    names the agent file and lists both models, and its setup state holds that
    endpoints entry with both models and that load error as what the write
    repairs. A change set for that agent file that sets the parallel limit of
    r9700/Qwen3.8 to 1 writes it, removes the endpoints map in the same write,
    and the agent file loads, with no parallel limit for r9700/Qwen3-4B.

    An agent file with one invalid agent entry gives a load error that names it
    and returns no agent definition, not even a valid one.

    A fallback agent that holds a description gives a load error that names it.
    With a general agent whose tool list holds read, write and bash and a
    fallback agent whose tool list holds only read, the fallback agent holds
    only read at every model tier, and general:medium keeps read, write and
    bash.

    With the model tiers small and large configured, the tier text small and
    tier:small map to small, the tier word cheapest capable maps to small, the
    tier word session model maps to large, the tier text anthropic/claude-opus
    is an unmatched tier text, and an empty tier text maps to no model tier.

    With the agent entry reviewer naming small as its own tier, a change set
    that removes small is refused naming reviewer, and nothing is written. A
    change set that gives the medium tier another model changes only the model
    of that tier, and every comment and every other line of the agent file
    stays as it was. A change set that moves the only model tier on the
    model r9700/Qwen3.8 to another model drops the parallel limit of
    r9700/Qwen3.8 in the same write. An apply after the agent file changed since
    its
    setup preview is refused with the stale preview error and writes nothing.

    With a tiers map that is a flow-style map and holds medium, small and
    large, a
    change set that removes small and large leaves only the medium tier, and
    the agent file loads. An apply whose write the file system refuses returns
    the write error and leaves the file as it was.

    A change set for the global file that sets the medium tier, in a project
    whose agent file holds its own medium tier, gives a setup preview that
    lists that medium tier as overridden by this project. A change set for the
    global file that removes large is refused when an agent entry of this
    project names large, and is not refused when the agent file of this project
    holds its own large. A change set for the agent file that sets only small,
    in a project with no agent file and a global file that holds a medium
    tier, writes only the tier small and no agent entry. A change set for the
    agent file that removes a model tier that only the global file holds
    writes a removal entry for it.
  }

  interface {
    Resolve an agent entry of the merged configuration by name at a requested
    model tier,
    at its own tier or at the medium tier, and provide the agent definition
    with its child host, its model, its thinking level, its tool list, its
    timeout, its model with the parallel limit of that model or none, and
    every skill folder resolved to an absolute folder. Fill each
    built-in agent from the starter tools that the caller gives, so that the
    general agent and the fallback agent resolve in a project that holds neither.

    Report which starter entries it added and which the agent file already
    held, and add the starter entries to the agent file under a project root
    that its caller hands over: the medium tier from a child host, a model and
    a thinking level, and the general agent and the fallback agent from the
    starter tools that the caller gives and a timeout, each only when the
    agent file does not hold it yet, with no skills, creating the agent file,
    its models map and its agents map when missing. Refuse with the load error
    when the file exists and does not load for a reason other than missing
    starter entries, and refuse with an error that names both child hosts,
    writing nothing, when the file already holds a medium tier whose child host
    differs from the child host that the caller gives, and refuse with an error
    that names the shared file, writing nothing, when the agent file is a shared
    file.

    Read the setup state of the merged configuration under a project root that
    its caller hands over, marking each model tier and each parallel limit
    with the file it comes from, giving each endpoints entry with its
    parallel limit and the models that belong to its model endpoint, so that a
    guided setup can offer that value as the parallel limit of each of those
    models, marking each model tier that runs on a child host other
    than the one the caller gives and, when the caller gives a list of models,
    each model tier whose model the list does not hold. Return the
    setup preview of a change set for the child host and the setup scope that
    the caller gives, and apply a change set with the
    preview fingerprint of its setup preview, writing the file of the setup
    scope only as that setup preview shows, or return the change set error, the
    stale preview error, the write error, each refused change or the
    load error and leave that file as it was.

    Provide the *child host options* of the global file for a child host, as
    written, to every caller that starts a child on that child host, also when
    the project is off or lacks a starter entry, so that guided setup can list
    and check a model that the child host options provide, and so that a child
    run, a model listing and a model check all get the same child host options.
    A guided setup is the one reader that reads the global file when the project
    is off, and a load error of that read refuses its whole model listing or
    model check.

    List the routable tier names, the medium tier among them, with the
    child host and the model each one runs for the general agent, apart from
    the names of every configured model tier.

    Map a tier text to a model tier, including each tier word, or report an
    unmatched tier text, and treat each agent alias as the general agent, so
    that every parent adapter maps a request the same way.

    Provide the routing block of the merged configuration, merged by key, so
    that a caller reads the hook switch and the generic agent list from the
    one place that merges them.

    List the names of every agent entry of the merged configuration, the
    built-in agents included, so that a caller can report them in an error.

    Return an *unknown agent error* that names the unknown name and every
    configured agent entry when a name is not configured, so that a front end
    can show it. When no agent is named, the error says that no agent was named
    and still lists every configured agent entry.

    Return the tier error that names the model tier an agent resolved to, every
    configured model tier and, when the child host is the cause, that
    child host and every supported child host, when the agent cannot run at
    that model tier.

    Return a load error that names the file it comes from and the invalid model
    tier or agent entry, the missing general agent, fallback agent or medium
    tier, or says that a file is unreadable or malformed, so that a front end
    can show it. Return the off error, which says whether the agent file is
    missing and the global file holds no medium tier or the enabled switch of
    the agent file is false, so that every caller treats the project as off and
    no subagent runs on a model that no file names.

    Provide the project root for a caller in the order the constraints give,
    starting from SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR, then the roots that the parent adapter
    reports, then the working directory of the command line, or return the
    unresolved root error when no source gives one.
  }

  decisions {
    Each skill folder resolves against the directory of the agent file and not the
    working directory, so the same file behaves the same from a parent adapter,
    the command line and the experiment harness.

    The child host, the model and the thinking level live in model tiers and
    not in agent entries, so that what runs a child is set once per tier and an
    agent says only what it does. An agent that needs something else at one
    tier gets a tier override for it, as a docker-compose override replaces
    fields of a base service, and an agent that should run on another tier by
    default names it as its own tier. The medium tier is required so that every
    agent entry resolves without a tier named, and the general agent and the
    fallback agent are required so that every request has an agent to run and a
    read-only agent for a mistyped name.

    The general agent and the fallback agent are entries of the agents map that
    are named general and fallback, so that a name lookup, a tier and an
    explicit request find them like any agent and no agent name is reserved.
    They have no description, because the general agent is the default for work
    that names no agent and for tiered work, and neither is chosen by its
    description or offered to a parent model for routing. The fallback agent
    takes the work of a name other than general that the agents map does not
    hold, and a front end's init writes it read-only so that a mistyped agent
    name cannot write to the repository or run shell commands, while tiered
    work keeps the tools of the general agent for compound-engineering
    reviewers. Only the child host knows which tool names only read, so the
    load cannot enforce read-only, and a person who edits the entry owns that
    change.

    The tier word and the tier text mapping live here and not in a
    parent adapter, so that the Claude Code parent host and the pi parent host
    map a request the same way. A project with no agent file and no medium tier
    in the global file gets the off error, which says to run subzero init, so
    that no subagent runs on a model that no file names.

    The global file lets a person set the model tiers once for every project,
    and the agent file lists only what differs, so an entry of the agent file
    replaces the global entry whole and a model never keeps a thinking level
    chosen for another model. The agent file is read first so that a project
    that switches itself off never depends on the global file, except for the
    guided setup, which reads the global file apart so that it can list and
    check models of a project that is off, and the global
    file holds no agent and no skill so that a file in a home folder cannot give
    every repository a tool list or a skill folder. The child host options
    are global-only, because they can name code that a child driver loads into
    every child, so a repository cannot add one through its agent file. The
    process environment is trusted, SUBZERO_GLOBAL_CONFIG included, because a
    repository that sets HOME or PATH can already run its own program, so
    guarding one variable would only look safe. The
    child host options are one map for each child host under the key hosts, read
    only by the child driver of that child host, so that AgentConfig and the
    core never name a child host and a later child host can have options of
    its own. A shared file is read as the
    global file so that
    one file never counts as both layers, and a change set for the agent file
    is refused there so that a key meant for one project is not written into
    the file of every project. The built-in agents mean a
    guided setup writes only the model tiers and limits that a person chose.

    A parallel limit keeps a fan-out on one model from flooding it. It is set
    for each model and not for each model endpoint, because a cloud provider
    limits each model and a local server can run each model in its own
    process, and one limit shared by several models on one server can no
    longer be written. The endpoints map is refused and not converted, because
    a limit shared by several models would silently become one limit for each
    of them, and guided setup offers the old value so that the person sees
    that change in its diff before anything is written.

    A change set edits the text of the file of its setup scope in place and
    does not render
    the document again, so that comments and lines it does not change survive.
    An apply is bound to its setup preview by the preview fingerprint, so that
    a person confirms exactly what is written, and the write is atomic because
    each delegation reads the agent file again on every call.
  }
}
